---
interface Props {
    currentProgress: number;
    totalGoal: number;
    label: string;
    unit: string;
}

const {currentProgress, totalGoal, label, unit} = Astro.props;

const percent = Math.max(0, Math.min(100, (currentProgress / totalGoal) * 100));

/**
 * A 2px RULE, NOT A PILL, and the change is one of job rather than of taste.
 *
 * This used to be a 24px capsule with the sport's icon riding the end of the fill: the
 * loudest object on the goal card, and the one carrying the least, because the figure
 * beside it already said how far and the card's own heading already said which sport.
 * What is left does exactly one thing — show a fraction — in the site's own structural
 * idiom, a hairline rule. The boldness it gave up went to the number above it, which is
 * the thing a visitor came to the card for.
 *
 * IT SPANS THE BODY, NOT THE FIGURE ABOVE IT. Sized to the text it underlines, it looks
 * better and it is wrong: the full scale becomes the number's own width, so cycling
 * (2279.7 / 5000) and running (158.6 / 600) would each be measuring against a different
 * 100% and the two cards could not be compared. The width is the constraint that makes
 * the mark mean something; do not trade it for the tighter look.
 *
 * THE TWO COLOURS ARE ITS OWN (--progress-track, --progress-fill) rather than --shadow
 * and a grey from the UnoCSS palette. That was a real defect: one shared #D4D4D4 track
 * served both themes, and in dark mode it stood at 12.09:1 against its card while the
 * fill managed 9.08:1 — the *empty* part of the bar was the strongest mark on it, so the
 * bar read backwards. See BasicLayout for the polarity rule the two tokens now encode.
 * That rule is the one thing here a redesign must not undo, and it is easier to undo now
 * that the mark is 2px: at this size the track is nearly a background, and a palette
 * change that leaves the fill quieter than it will not look obviously wrong in either
 * theme while still being the same defect.
 *
 * SC 1.4.11 STILL APPLIES. It is a graphical object that conveys information, so the
 * fill is held to 3:1 against the track it sits in. It is no longer held against the ink
 * that used to ride it: the icon is gone, and with it the reason --on-brand existed on
 * this element. Put a glyph back and that obligation returns — it was at 1.89:1 in dark
 * mode before the token was introduced.
 *
 * THE TWO COLOURS STAY IN UTILITY CLASSES rather than moving in beside
 * the geometry, and that is a testability decision worth keeping. tests/build-output.test.ts
 * resolves each element's painted background out of the SHIPPED stylesheet by class token
 * — which is what lets it assert the polarity rule above, that the fill is the region
 * standing further from the card, and that neither colour has been quietly re-coupled to
 * a token belonging to something else. Move these into `<style>` and four assertions stop
 * seeing anything.
 *
 * The fill no longer clips its own overflow. That clip existed to stop the glyph painting
 * on bare track at low progress, and with the glyph gone it guarded nothing; the track
 * still clips, which is the structural guarantee that a fill can never paint outside the
 * bar whatever its width resolves to.
 *
 * Utility names are deliberately not written literally anywhere in this file's prose:
 * UnoCSS reads comments and emits a rule for anything that parses as one.
 */
---

<div
        class="measure overflow-hidden bg-[var(--progress-track)]"
        role="progressbar"
        aria-label={label}
        aria-valuemin="0"
        aria-valuemax={totalGoal}
        aria-valuenow={currentProgress}
        aria-valuetext={`${currentProgress} of ${totalGoal} ${unit}`}
>
    <div class="progress-fill bg-[var(--progress-fill)]" style={`--progress: ${percent}%`}></div>
</div>

<style>
    /*
     * A pinned 2px rather than a font-relative height, and it is the one length on this
     * card that should be pinned. Every other measurement here holds text off other text
     * and has to scale with the reader's; this is the stroke weight of a drawn line, which
     * is a hairline at every text size or it is not a hairline. It cannot clip anything
     * either — there is no ink inside it.
     */
    .measure {
        height: 2px;
    }

    .progress-fill {
        height: 100%;
        width: var(--progress);
        animation: progress-grow 1.5s ease-in-out 0.3s backwards;
    }

    @keyframes progress-grow {
        from {
            width: 0;
        }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .progress-fill {
            animation: none;
        }
    }

    /*
     * Forced colours drop `background` on both halves, so the whole rule would vanish and
     * take the only drawn indication of progress with it. The pair the mode reserves for
     * a filled indicator is Highlight on Canvas, and a border is what keeps the track's
     * extent legible where Canvas matches the page.
     */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .measure {
            border: 1px solid CanvasText;
            box-sizing: content-box;
        }

        .progress-fill {
            forced-color-adjust: none;
            background: Highlight;
        }
    }
</style>
